# Motivation # {#motivation}

`char8_t`’s underlying type is `unsigned char` (<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.types#basic.fundamental-9.sentence-1">[basic.fundamental]</a>),
yet `std::underlying_type_t<char8_t>` is ill-formed.

`char32_t`’s underlying type is `uint32_t`. On a 32-bit platform, it is permitted for `uint32_t` to be `unsigned long`.
Yet on such a platform `make_unsigned_t<char32_t>` will still be `unsigned int`, not `unsigned long`.

A user-hostile implementation might give `long` 32 value bits, and give `int` 16 value bits and 16 padding bits, so that
`sizeof(int) == sizeof(long)`. Then declare `enum E : long {}` and discover that `make_signed_t<E>` is `int`, not `long`,
because `make_signed_t` doesn't care about the number of value bits, only the size
(<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/meta.trans.sign">[meta.trans.sign]</a>).

For all these reasons but especially the last one, [[P3643]] defines its own helper <i><code>underlying-int-t&lt;T&gt;</code></i>
and writes

    template<class T>
      constexpr make_signed_t&lt;underlying-int-t&lt;T>> to_signed(T value) noexcept;

where we would have preferred to see

    template&lt;class T>
      constexpr make_signed_t&lt;T> to_signed(T value) noexcept;

The first goal of this paper is to bring `make_signed` and `make_unsigned` into harmony with [[P3643]]'s proposed `to_signed` semantics,
so that `make_signed_t<T>` is always equivalent to `decltype(to_signed(T()))`.

The second goal of this paper is to bring `underlying_type` into harmony with the core language's multiple meanings
of "underlying type," such that for any type `T`, `underlying_type_t<T>` should return `T`’s underlying type if `T` has one.

## Design considerations ## {#design-considerations}

Q: What if `enum T : char8_t`? Should `underlying_type_t<T>` be `char8_t` or `unsigned char`?

A: For an enum type, usually the goal of `underlying_type_t<T>` is to get down to an integral type we can do math on;
which suggests we should keep looking through every "layer" until we get to the most fundamental bottom layer. However,
C++11-to-present has already decided that `underlying_type_t<T>` should be `char8_t`; let's not gratuitously break that
behavior without a good reason.

Q: Should `make_signed_t<bool>` be defined to `signed char` and `make_unsigned_t<bool>` be defined to `unsigned char`?

A: I think not. `make_signed` and P3643’s proposed `to_signed` both forbid calling them with `bool` (or, since [[LWG4634]],
enum types with underlying type `bool`).



(2) While `underlying_type_t<T>` is invariably an integer type
(<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/dcl.enum#2.sentence-4">[dcl.enum]</a>), it can be `bool`. `bool` is neither a signed integer type
nor an unsigned integer type, although `is_unsigned_v<bool>` is `true`. `bool` has no underlying type, although it has a similar special
relationship to an implementation-defined unsigned integer type (<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.fundamental#10.sentence-1">[basic.fundamental]</a>).
Because of (2), our answer to (1) is that `underlying_type_t` should strip just one layer, not all layers.



| T | Underlying type | std::underlying_type_t<T> | std::make_signed_t<T> | std::make_unsigned_t<T> |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| bool | — | — | — | — |
| char | unsigned char | — | signed char | unsigned char |
| char8_t | unsigned char | — | signed char | unsigned char |



<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Underlying type</th><th>underlying_type_t&lt;T&gt;</th><th>make_signed_t&lt;T&gt;</th></tr>
<tr><td>char</td>signed char</tr>

# Proposed wording # {#wording}

## [meta.trans.sign] ## {#wording-meta.trans.sign}

Modify the table in <a href="http://eel.is/c++draft/meta.trans.sign">[meta.trans.sign]</a> as follows:

<small><blockquote>
<p><pre>
template&lt;class T>
struct make_signed;
</pre>
<p>If `T` is a (possibly _cv_-qualified) signed integer type ([basic.fundamental]) then the member typedef `type` denotes `T`;
otherwise, if `T` is a (possibly _cv_-qualified) unsigned integer type then `type` denotes the corresponding signed integer type,
with the same _cv_-qualifiers as `T`; otherwise, `type` denotes the signed integer type with smallest rank ([conv.rank])
for which `sizeof(T) == sizeof(type)`, with the same _cv_-qualifiers as `T`.
<p><i>Mandates:</i> `T` is an integral or enumeration type other than _cv_ `bool`.
<p><pre>
template&lt;class T>
struct make_unsigned;
</pre>
<p>If `T` is a (possibly _cv_-qualified) unsigned integer type ([basic.fundamental]) then the member typedef `type` denotes `T`;
otherwise, if `T` is a (possibly cv-qualified) signed integer type then `type` denotes the corresponding unsigned integer type,
with the same _cv_-qualifiers as `T`; otherwise, `type` denotes the unsigned integer type with smallest rank ([conv.rank])
for which `sizeof(T) == sizeof(type)`, with the same _cv_-qualifiers as `T`.
<i>Mandates:</i> `T` is an integral or enumeration type other than _cv_ `bool`.
</blockquote></small>

## [meta.trans.other] ## {#wording-meta.trans.other}

Modify the table in <a href="http://eel.is/c++draft/meta.trans.other">[meta.trans.other]</a> as follows:

<small><blockquote>
<p><pre>
template&lt;class T>
struct underlying_type;
</pre>
<p>If `T` is a<del>n</del> <ins>character or</ins> enumeration type, the member typedef `type` denotes the underlying type of `T` (<ins>[basic.fundamental],</ins> [dcl.enum]);
otherwise, there is no member `type`.
<p><i>Mandates:</i> `T` is not an incomplete enumeration type.
</blockquote></small>

## [utility.underlying] (no change) ## {#wording-utility.underlying}

Don't modify <a href="http://eel.is/c++draft/utility.underlying">[utility.underlying]</a>.

<small><blockquote>
<p><pre>
  template&lt;class T>
    constexpr underlying_type_t&lt;T> to_underlying(T value) noexcept;
</pre>
<p>1․ Returns: `static_cast<underlying_type_t<T>>(value)`.
</blockquote></small>


